from pathlib import Path
from runpy import run_path
g=run_path(str(Path(__file__).with_name('author-tools-generator.py')))
L=g['lesson']; C=g['course']
def J(*args): return L(*args, language='javascript')
def T(*args): return L(*args, language='typescript')

C('data-structures-algorithms','A data structure is a way to arrange information. An algorithm is a set of steps that solves a problem. We will use small lists and simple JavaScript to see both ideas at work.','The JavaScript examples run in this site. Read the JavaScript basics first if variables and loops are new to you.','https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Guide/Indexed_collections',[
J('Steps and stored data','An algorithm is like a recipe. A list stores several values in order. Here the steps add three scores, one at a time.','const scores = [2, 4, 6];\nlet total = 0;\nfor (const score of scores) total += score;\nconsole.log(total);','The result is 12: 2 plus 4 plus 6. The list holds the input; the loop gives the steps.','Add a score of 3 and predict the new total.','Start the total at zero before adding the scores.','What is the total of 2, 4 and 6?','12','246'),
J('Array positions','An array is an ordered list. Its first position is zero. A position is called an index.','const fruit = ["apple", "pear", "mango"];\nconsole.log(fruit[1]);','The result is pear. Index 1 is the second place in the list.','Read index 2 instead.','Index 3 is outside this three-item array.','Which index selects the first item?','0','1'),
J('Stacks','A stack removes the newest item first. Think of a pile of plates: you lift the top plate first.','const stack = [];\nstack.push("red");\nstack.push("blue");\nconsole.log(stack.pop());','The result is blue. It was added last and removed first.','Add green before removing an item.','Do not use a stack when the oldest request must go first.','Which stack operation removes the newest item?','pop','push'),
J('Queues','A queue serves the oldest item first. Think of a line at the library. This tiny example uses an array.','const queue = ["Asha", "Ben"];\nqueue.push("Cara");\nconsole.log(queue.shift());','The result is Asha. Cara joins the back of the queue.','Serve the next learner with another shift.','Array shift can be slow for very large queues because other items move.','Who is served first in this queue?','Asha','Cara'),
J('Sets','A set stores each value once. It is useful when you want a list without repeated values.','const colors = new Set(["red", "blue", "red"]);\nconsole.log(colors.size);','The result is 2 because red appears only once in the set.','Add blue again and check the size.','Two different object values are not merged just because their fields match.','How many different colors are stored?','2','3'),
J('Maps','A map pairs a key with a value. A key is a label used to find a value, like a name on a locker.','const scores = new Map();\nscores.set("Asha", 8);\nscores.set("Ben", 6);\nconsole.log(scores.get("Asha"));','The result is 8. The key Asha points to her score.','Change Asha to 9 with set, then read it.','Setting the same key again replaces its old value.','What does get("Asha") return?','8','Asha'),
J('Linear search','Linear search checks items one by one until it finds a match. It also works on an unsorted list.','const values = [7, 2, 9];\nlet found = -1;\nfor (let i = 0; i < values.length; i++) {\n  if (values[i] === 9) { found = i; break; }\n}\nconsole.log(found);','The result is 2, the index of 9. A result of -1 would mean no match was found.','Search for 5 and explain the result.','Do not confuse the index of a value with the value itself.','What does -1 mean in this search?','No match was found','The first item matched'),
J('Binary search','Binary search cuts a sorted search area in half. It compares the middle item, then keeps only the useful half.','const a = [2, 4, 6, 8, 10];\nlet low = 0, high = a.length - 1, found = -1;\nwhile (low <= high) {\n  const mid = Math.floor((low + high) / 2);\n  if (a[mid] === 8) { found = mid; break; }\n  if (a[mid] < 8) low = mid + 1;\n  else high = mid - 1;\n}\nconsole.log(found);','The result is 3. Searching for 8 first checks 6 and then searches the right half.','Search for 2 and trace low and high on paper.','Binary search needs the values in sorted order.','What must be true before this search starts?','The list is sorted','The list is shuffled'),
J('Sorting numbers','Sorting arranges values in order. JavaScript needs a number comparison when you want number order.','const values = [10, 2, 6];\nconst sorted = [...values].sort((a, b) => a - b);\nconsole.log(sorted.join(", "));','The result is 2, 6, 10. The spread makes a copy, so values is unchanged.','Reverse the comparison to b - a.','Without the comparison, sort treats values like words.','Why is a - b passed to sort?','To compare numbers','To add every number'),
J('Counting work','We can compare algorithms by how their work grows. One loop over n items does about n visits. This is called linear work, written O(n).','const values = [3, 4, 5, 6];\nlet visits = 0;\nfor (const value of values) visits++;\nconsole.log(visits);','The result is 4. Twice as many items would mean twice as many visits.','Add two values and count the visits.','O(n) describes growth; it is not a promise of an exact running time.','How many visits does this loop make?','4','16'),
J('Trees','A tree joins parent items to child items. Folders inside folders form a tree. A leaf has no children.','const tree = { name: "school", children: [\n  { name: "art", children: [] },\n  { name: "music", children: [] }\n] };\nconsole.log(tree.children.map(child => child.name).join(", "));','The result is art, music. School is the parent; art and music are leaves.','Add a sports leaf and read the names again.','A tree has no path that loops back to an earlier parent.','Which item is the root of this tree?','school','art'),
J('Graphs and neighbours','A graph stores connections between items. Unlike a tree, a graph may have loops. A neighbour is directly connected to an item.','const paths = { gate: ["hall"], hall: ["gate", "library"], library: ["hall"] };\nconsole.log(paths.hall.join(", "));','The result is gate, library. Both are one step from the hall.','Add a garden connected to the gate in both directions.','When exploring a graph, remember visited items so a loop does not trap you.','Which room is directly linked to the library?','hall','garden'),
])

C('object-oriented-programming','Object-oriented programming groups data and useful actions together. We will build tiny JavaScript objects for a library and see how classes help us make more than one object.','These JavaScript examples run here. A class is a tool for organising code; a small program does not always need one.','https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Learn_web_development/Extensions/Advanced_JavaScript_objects',[
J('Objects hold related facts','An object keeps related values together. A property is one named value inside the object.','const book = { title: "Moon", pages: 24 };\nconsole.log(book.title);','The result is Moon. The title property belongs to book.','Add an author property and print it.','Put text values in quotes. Property names and values have different jobs.','Which property stores 24?','pages','title'),
J('Methods perform actions','A method is a function stored on an object. It can use this to read the object that called it.','const book = { title: "Moon", describe() { return this.title + " book"; } };\nconsole.log(book.describe());','The result is Moon book. The parentheses call the method.','Change the title and call describe again.','An arrow function does not create its own this value. Use the method form shown here.','What do parentheses after describe do?','Call the method','Delete the method'),
J('Classes are plans','A class is a plan for making similar objects. Each object made from it is called an instance.','class Book {\n  constructor(title) { this.title = title; }\n}\nconst book = new Book("Ocean");\nconsole.log(book.title);','The result is Ocean. new makes an instance and runs the constructor.','Make a second Book with another title.','Call this class with new; it is not an ordinary function call.','What does new Book make?','An instance','A new programming language'),
J('Independent instances','Each instance has its own properties. Changing one book does not change a different book.','class Book { constructor(title) { this.title = title; } }\nconst a = new Book("Moon");\nconst b = new Book("Ocean");\na.title = "Stars";\nconsole.log(b.title);','The result is Ocean. The two objects hold separate title values.','Print a.title too.','Assigning b = a would make both names refer to the same object.','Does changing a.title change b.title here?','No','Yes'),
J('Constructors set a starting state','A constructor sets up a new object. A default value is used when the caller leaves out an argument.','class Counter {\n  constructor(start = 0) { this.value = start; }\n}\nconsole.log(new Counter().value);','The result is 0 because no starting value was supplied.','Make new Counter(5) and print its value.','Validate inputs when real callers might pass wrong kinds of values.','Which starting value is used when none is supplied?','0','undefined'),
J('Keeping data private','A private field starts with #. Code outside the class cannot directly read or change it.','class Counter {\n  #value = 0;\n  add() { this.#value++; }\n  read() { return this.#value; }\n}\nconst c = new Counter();\nc.add();\nconsole.log(c.read());','The result is 1. Callers use add and read to work with the hidden value.','Call add twice before read.','Private fields help organise access; they do not turn browser data into a secure online account.','Which method reads the private count?','read','add'),
J('Getters provide a value','A getter calculates a value when you read a property. You do not call it with parentheses.','class Square {\n  constructor(side) { this.side = side; }\n  get area() { return this.side * this.side; }\n}\nconsole.log(new Square(3).area);','The result is 9. area multiplies the side by itself.','Use a side of 4 and predict the area.','Read .area, not .area(), because this member is a getter.','How is the getter read?','.area','.area()'),
J('Inheritance adds to a class','Inheritance lets one class extend another. The child class receives methods from its parent.','class Animal { speak() { return "sound"; } }\nclass Dog extends Animal {}\nconsole.log(new Dog().speak());','The result is sound. Dog uses the inherited speak method.','Add a name property in Animal using a constructor.','Use inheritance only when the child really is a kind of the parent.','Which keyword creates a child class?','extends','returns'),
J('Overriding a method','A child can replace an inherited method with its own version. This is called overriding.','class Animal { speak() { return "sound"; } }\nclass Dog extends Animal { speak() { return "woof"; } }\nconsole.log(new Dog().speak());','The result is woof. Dog supplies its own speak method.','Add a Cat class whose speak method returns meow.','Keep the method purpose clear when replacing it in a child.','Which speak method runs for new Dog()?','Dog speak','Animal speak'),
J('Polymorphism uses one common action','Polymorphism means different objects can answer the same method call. The caller uses the action without choosing each object type.','const sounds = [\n  { speak() { return "woof"; } },\n  { speak() { return "meow"; } }\n];\nconsole.log(sounds.map(animal => animal.speak()).join(", "));','The result is woof, meow. Each object handles speak in its own way.','Add a duck object with speak.','Each object in this list must provide speak before you call it.','What action do both objects support?','speak','fly'),
J('Composition builds from parts','Composition gives an object another object to use. A bike has a bell; a bike is not a kind of bell.','const bell = { ring() { return "ding"; } };\nconst bike = { bell, signal() { return this.bell.ring(); } };\nconsole.log(bike.signal());','The result is ding. The bike asks its bell to do one job.','Replace the bell with one that returns ring-ring.','Do not make Bike extend Bell just to borrow ring.','Which relationship describes this design?','A bike has a bell','A bike is a bell'),
J('Give each class a clear job','A class is easier to understand when it has one clear job. A basket can add prices without also drawing the whole web page.','class Basket {\n  prices = [];\n  add(price) { this.prices.push(price); }\n  total() { return this.prices.reduce((sum, price) => sum + price, 0); }\n}\nconst basket = new Basket();\nbasket.add(3); basket.add(4);\nconsole.log(basket.total());','The result is 7. Basket handles prices; another part of the app can show the total.','Add a third price and check the total.','A class with many unrelated jobs becomes hard to change and test.','What is Basket responsible for here?','Adding and totalling prices','Sending emails'),
])

C('design-patterns','A design pattern is a reusable idea for arranging code. It is not code you must copy everywhere. We will compare small JavaScript examples and learn when each idea helps.','Run the JavaScript examples here. Choose a pattern only when it makes a real problem easier to understand.','https://refactoring.guru/design-patterns',[
J('Patterns solve repeated problems','A pattern gives a name to a useful design idea. Before choosing one, describe the job the code must do.','const job = { input: 3, action: n => n * 2 };\nconsole.log(job.action(job.input));','The result is 6. A tiny function already solves this job; no extra class is needed.','Change the action to add 2.','Adding a pattern without a problem can make simple code harder.','Should every small function use a design pattern?','No, use one when it helps','Yes, always add classes'),
J('Factory functions','A factory makes objects. It keeps object creation in one place so callers do not repeat the setup.','function makeBook(title) { return { title, borrowed: false }; }\nconsole.log(makeBook("Moon").borrowed);','The call makeBook("Moon") passes Moon into the title parameter. The returned object has title: "Moon" and borrowed: false. Writing .borrowed reads that one property, so the console prints false. Each call returns a fresh book object.','Make two books and change borrowed on only one.','Return a fresh object when callers need independent objects.','What does this factory return?','A new book object','The same title string only'),
J('Strategy functions','A strategy is a swappable way to do a job. Pass a function when the rule may change.','function price(total, discount) { return discount(total); }\nconst halfPrice = value => value / 2;\nconsole.log(price(10, halfPrice));','The result is 5. price uses the supplied discount rule.','Pass a rule that subtracts 2.','Do not hide unrelated side effects inside a strategy function.','What can change without rewriting price?','The discount rule','The JavaScript language'),
J('Observer notifications','An observer listens for a change. The subject tells its listeners when something happens.','const listeners = [];\nlisteners.push(value => console.log("Score " + value));\nfor (const notify of listeners) notify(8);','The result is Score 8. The listener receives the new value.','Add a second listener that prints twice the score.','Remove listeners when they are no longer needed to avoid repeated work.','What does the listener receive?','The new score','A secret password'),
J('Adapters translate interfaces','An adapter changes one form of input or output into another. It lets two parts work together without changing both.','const oldWeather = { fahrenheit: 68 };\nfunction asCelsius(weather) { return (weather.fahrenheit - 32) * 5 / 9; }\nconsole.log(asCelsius(oldWeather));','The result is 20. The adapter converts Fahrenheit to Celsius.','Try 32 degrees Fahrenheit.','Check units before passing a value to another part of a program.','What is the adapter changing?','The temperature unit','The weather outside'),
J('Decorators add behaviour','A decorator wraps a function with extra behaviour. The original function can stay focused on its job.','const greet = name => "Hi " + name;\nconst excited = fn => name => fn(name) + "!";\nconsole.log(excited(greet)("Asha"));','The result is Hi Asha!. The wrapper adds the final mark.','Make a wrapper that adds a star before the greeting.','Too many wrappers can hide the order in which work happens.','Which part adds the exclamation mark?','The wrapper','The name'),
J('Facades offer a small doorway','A facade gives callers a simple way to use several parts. It hides the order of small steps behind one clear action.','const readTitle = () => "Moon";\nconst readPages = () => 24;\nfunction bookSummary() { return readTitle() + ": " + readPages() + " pages"; }\nconsole.log(bookSummary());','The result is Moon: 24 pages. The caller only needs bookSummary.','Add an author to the summary using another helper.','A facade should simplify access, not silently change what the helpers mean.','What does a facade offer?','A simpler way to use several parts','A copy of every file'),
J('Commands store actions','A command packages an action so it can be saved in a queue and run later.','let score = 0;\nconst addPoint = { execute() { score++; } };\nconst queue = [addPoint, addPoint];\nqueue.forEach(command => command.execute());\nconsole.log(score);','The result is 2. Two commands each add a point.','Add a command that removes a point.','Saving a command is different from running it.','When does score change?','When execute is called','When the command is named'),
J('State changes allowed behaviour','State describes the current condition of an object. Different states can allow different actions.','let state = "closed";\nfunction openDoor() { if (state === "closed") state = "open"; }\nopenDoor();\nconsole.log(state);','The result is open. The rule allows a closed door to open.','Add a closeDoor function that works only when open.','List the allowed state changes instead of accepting every action in every state.','Which state does openDoor produce?','open','locked'),
J('Builders collect choices','A builder collects choices before producing a result. It helps when creation has several optional parts.','const choices = { title: "Moon", color: "blue" };\nfunction buildCard(options) { return options.title + " / " + (options.color ?? "white"); }\nconsole.log(buildCard(choices));','The result is Moon / blue. The default color is used only when no color is supplied.','Build a card with a title but no color.','Do not use a large builder when one short function is clearer.','Which color is used if color is missing?','white','blue'),
J('Shared state needs care','A single shared object can be convenient, but every caller sees its changes. This makes hidden links between features.','const settings = { sound: true };\nconst first = settings;\nconst second = settings;\nfirst.sound = false;\nconsole.log(second.sound);','The result is false. Both names refer to the same object.','Use a copied object for second and compare the result.','A shared object is not automatically a good design just because there is only one.','Why does second.sound change?','Both names refer to one object','False is a random result'),
J('Choose the smallest useful design','Compare designs by clarity and the changes you expect. A function, a plain object or a pattern can each be the right choice.','const discounts = { none: n => n, half: n => n / 2 };\nconsole.log(discounts.half(12));','The result is 6. A small strategy table is enough for these two rules.','Add a rule named saveTwo.','Do not force all patterns into one project. Use only the ones that solve a clear need.','Why is this small strategy table useful?','It keeps changeable rules together','It makes all errors impossible'),
])

C('system-design','System design plans how parts of a service work together. We will use a school library as a worked example. The plans explain requests, data and failures without building a server.','These are paper plans and small local calculations. Reading this course needs no cloud account. The learning website itself remains a frontend-only app.','https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/guide/',[
L('Begin with the goal','A requirement says what a system must do. Separate the learner action from a quality goal such as speed.','Action: find a library book by title\nQuality goal: show a result within 2 seconds\nNot included: selling books','The plan has one main action and one goal we can measure. Keeping sales out makes the first design smaller.','Write one action and one quality goal for a lunch menu app.','Do not choose tools before you know the problem.','Which line is a quality goal?','Show a result within 2 seconds','Selling books'),
L('Follow one request','A request asks another part for work. A response is the reply. Draw one complete trip before adding more parts.','Browser -> book search service -> catalog data\nBrowser <- search results <- matching books','The browser asks for a title. The service reads the catalog and sends matches back.','Draw the same trip for finding a book by author.','A diagram arrow should say who sends the request and who replies.','What is a response?','A reply to a request','A second database'),
L('Choose the data','A data model lists the facts you need and how they relate. A stable ID distinguishes books that have the same title.','Book: id=17, title=Moon, author=Asha\nCopy: id=203, bookId=17, shelf=B2\nCopy: id=204, bookId=17, shelf=B3','Two physical copies point to one book record. A title alone cannot tell the copies apart.','Add a third copy on shelf C1.','Do not use a changeable title as the only identity for a book.','What links each copy to its book?','bookId','shelf alone'),
L('Estimate demand','An estimate is a rough calculation using clear assumptions. It helps you spot an obviously too-small design.','600 learners\nEach makes 5 searches per day\n600 x 5 = 3000 searches per day','The estimate is 3000 daily searches. It does not tell us the busiest second of the day.','Calculate the daily total for 800 learners.','A daily average can hide a lunchtime rush.','How many searches are estimated each day?','3000','600'),
L('Latency and throughput','Latency is how long one request takes. Throughput is how many requests finish in a period of time.','Request A takes 200 milliseconds\nThe system finishes 30 requests in one second','The first line measures waiting time. The second measures amount of work completed.','Write a slower latency and a higher throughput as two separate facts.','A system can finish many requests while some users still wait too long.','Which measure describes one learner waiting?','Latency','Throughput'),
L('Caches keep useful copies','A cache is a saved copy used to answer repeated requests faster. It may become old when the original data changes.','First title search: read catalog, save result in cache\nNext same search: use cached result\nBook renamed: remove or refresh saved result','The cache avoids repeating some work. It needs a rule for refreshing old answers.','Decide whether a book description or a live loan count can stay cached longer.','Do not assume a cached answer always matches the latest data.','What can happen to cached data?','It can become out of date','It automatically becomes more accurate'),
L('Indexes help find records','An index is a shortcut from a value to a record. It is like the word list at the back of a textbook.','Title index:\nMoon -> book 17\nOcean -> book 22\nZoo -> book 31','Searching the index can avoid reading every record. The index takes space and must be updated with changes.','Add a new title and its book ID to the index.','An index helps selected searches; adding one for every field has a cost.','What does an index trade for faster searches?','Extra space and update work','No cost at all'),
L('Queues handle work later','A queue holds jobs waiting to be done. It can keep a slow task from delaying the first reply.','User asks for a reading report\nService queues report job and returns job 52\nWorker makes report later\nUser checks job 52','The first reply says the task was accepted, not that the report is ready.','Draw what happens if the worker fails and the job must be retried.','Do not say a queued job is finished before a worker completes it.','What does the first reply confirm?','The job was accepted','The report is ready'),
L('Copies improve availability','Availability means the system can answer when needed. Extra copies of a service can help when one copy stops working.','Search copy A: unavailable\nSearch copy B: healthy\nRequest router sends new work to B','The healthy copy handles new requests. The router needs a way to check each copy.','Add a third copy and mark which ones are healthy.','Copies do not help if all of them depend on the same failed part.','Why keep another healthy service copy?','To keep answering after one failure','To remove the need for data'),
L('Consistency describes agreement','Consistency asks whether readers see the agreed current data. Copies may briefly disagree while a change travels between them.','Copy A: book 17 is borrowed\nCopy B: book 17 is available\nUpdate still travelling from A to B','A learner reading B may see an old answer. For a real loan, the final decision must use a rule that prevents lending the same copy twice.','Name one screen where a slightly old answer is acceptable.','Do not promise both instant agreement and uninterrupted service under every network failure.','What is the risk of reading B now?','Seeing an old availability value','Changing the book title'),
L('Retries need safe rules','A retry repeats a request after a problem. An idempotent action has the same effect when the same request is repeated.','Request ID: loan-52\nFirst request: create loan and remember loan-52\nRetry with loan-52: return the existing loan','The same request does not create two loans. The service remembers enough to recognise it.','Explain why retrying a payment without an ID can be dangerous.','A timeout does not prove the first request failed to take effect.','What prevents a retry from making a second loan?','Remembering the request ID','Waiting a random amount of time'),
L('Observe and review the design','A metric is a number you track. A log records an event. Use both to discover problems and review a design.','Metric: 5 searches failed in the last minute\nLog: request 52 could not reach catalog\nReview: check catalog health and recent changes','The metric shows the size of the problem; the log gives a clue about one event.','Write a log line that helps without including a learner name or password.','Do not put secret keys or unnecessary personal data into logs.','Which tool records a particular event?','A log','A daily average only'),
])

C('microservices','Microservices divide a larger application into small services with clear jobs. We will plan a library example and learn the extra costs of communication, testing and failures.','This is a design course using worked examples. It does not add services to this website. A small project is often easier as one application.','https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/microservices/',[
L('Small services with clear jobs','A service is a part that does work for another part. Microservices divide an app by jobs, such as catalog search and lending.','Catalog service: book titles and authors\nLoan service: who borrowed each copy\nBrowser: shows results to a learner','The jobs are separated by purpose. Merely splitting files does not create useful service boundaries.','Choose two jobs in a school canteen system.','Many tiny services can be harder to manage than one clear application.','What should guide a service boundary?','A clear business job','One service for every line of code'),
L('Own the data you change','A service should clearly own the data it changes. Other services use its interface rather than editing its tables directly.','Catalog owns Book records\nLoans owns Loan records\nLoans asks Catalog whether book 17 exists','Catalog remains responsible for book facts. Loans stays responsible for lending facts.','Decide which service changes a book title.','Shared writable tables create hidden links between services.','Who should update a book title?','Catalog','Every service at once'),
L('Contracts explain requests','A contract describes the request fields and response fields. Both sides need to agree on their meaning.','Request: GET /books/17\nResponse: {"id":17,"title":"Moon"}\nMissing book: status 404','The contract tells callers what a successful result and a missing item look like.','Write a response for book 22 titled Ocean.','Do not change a field meaning without telling callers.','What does 404 mean in this contract?','The book was not found','The book has 404 pages'),
L('Direct calls can wait','In a direct call, the caller waits for a reply. This is simple to follow but the caller depends on the other service responding.','Loans -> Catalog: is book 17 known?\nCatalog -> Loans: yes\nLoans continues','Loans cannot finish this step until Catalog replies or a time limit is reached.','Add a reply for an unknown book.','Always plan for slow or missing replies.','Which service is waiting in this example?','Loans','The database index'),
L('Events announce something happened','An event reports a past fact. Other services may react to it without the sender waiting for every reaction.','Event: BookBorrowed\nFields: eventId=81, copyId=203\nReport service updates a daily count','The event says a loan already happened. It is different from asking whether a loan should happen.','Name an event for a returned book.','Choose names that describe a fact, not a vague request.','Does BookBorrowed describe the past or ask permission?','It describes a past fact','It asks for permission'),
L('Duplicate messages are possible','Message delivery can repeat. A consumer should recognise an event it has already handled.','Receive event 81: increase count; remember 81\nReceive event 81 again: ignore duplicate\nCount increases once','Remembering handled IDs avoids double counting. The storage and cleanup rule for IDs must be planned.','Trace events 81, 82, 81 and count the new events.','Do not assume every event arrives exactly once.','How many times should event 81 change the count?','Once','Every time it is received'),
L('Timeouts bound waiting','A timeout sets a limit on waiting. The caller must choose a useful response when that limit passes.','Request begins at 0 ms\nTimeout limit: 800 ms\nNo reply at 800 ms -> show a retry message','The caller stops waiting after the limit. The remote work might still be running.','Choose a clear user message for a timed-out search.','A timeout does not cancel all work in another service automatically.','What does a timeout limit?','How long the caller waits','The number of book titles'),
L('Circuit breakers pause failing calls','A circuit breaker stops repeated calls to a service that is failing. Later it allows a small trial to see whether recovery happened.','Several Catalog calls fail\nBreaker opens: new calls fail fast\nAfter a pause: allow one trial call','Failing fast avoids building a long queue of waiting calls. The trial helps detect recovery.','Draw open, trial and closed states.','A circuit breaker does not repair the failed service.','Why stop calls briefly?','To avoid piling up failing work','To erase failed requests from history'),
L('Work across services needs recovery','A multi-step business action can partly succeed. A recovery action, often called compensation, tries to undo an earlier step.','1. Reserve a book copy\n2. Record the loan\nIf step 2 fails: release the reservation','Releasing the reservation lets another learner borrow the copy. Recovery can also fail and needs monitoring.','Write a recovery step for reserving then cancelling a lunch order.','Do not assume several services share one automatic database transaction.','What is the recovery action here?','Release the reservation','Reserve a second book'),
L('Versions protect callers','A new contract version gives callers time to move. A safe added optional field is often easier than removing a field callers still need.','Old response: {"id":17,"title":"Moon"}\nNew response: {"id":17,"title":"Moon","pages":24}','A caller that ignores unknown fields can keep using id and title. Removing title would break it.','Describe how to retire a field only after callers stop using it.','Adding a field can still break strict callers; check their contracts.','Which change is more likely to break an old caller?','Removing title','Keeping title unchanged'),
L('Trace a request across parts','A trace follows one request through several services. A shared trace ID connects the records.','trace-7 Browser -> Catalog\ntrace-7 Catalog -> Search index\ntrace-7 Search index took 900 ms','The matching ID helps find where time was spent. It should not contain private learner information.','Add a Loans step to the same trace.','Unrelated log lines are hard to join without a shared ID.','What joins these trace records?','trace-7','The book title'),
L('Choose services only when needed','Microservices bring independent releases and scaling, but add network calls and more operations work. Compare those costs before splitting an app.','Small team, one release schedule -> one well-organised app\nMany teams, different scaling needs -> consider service boundaries','Both choices can be good. The team and problem decide; a fashionable name does not.','List one benefit and one cost of separating search from loans.','Do not turn a static tutorial website into a backend project just to practise architecture.','What should decide the architecture?','The actual needs and costs','The newest buzzword'),
])
